104. Maximum Depth of Binary Tree

Given the root of a binary tree, return its maximum depth.
A binary tree's maximum depth is the number of nodes along the longest path from the root node down to the farthest leaf node.

Explanation:

FIrst, have a arraylist to store array.
Then have a queue to put all the traversed elements in it, so that as per the numbers in the queue we can traverse its children nodes.
first put the root node in queue.
then hve an array called level to store the children elements.
for every levelkeep adding depth++;

So take the curr by treenode cur = queue.poll()
this is the element out of queue, then check the left and right children nodes, and add them to the q.

fianlly depth is the total depth of the tree

depth is ourside because we are doing this untill q is empty.

Code:

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode() {}
 *     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if(root==null){
            return 0;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);

        int depth = 0;

        while(!q.isEmpty()){
            int size = q.size();
            List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
            depth++;
            for(int i=0;i<size;i++){
                TreeNode curr = q.poll();
                level.add(curr.val);

                if(curr.left!=null){
                    q.offer(curr.left);
                }
                if(curr.right!=null){
                    q.offer(curr.right);
                }
            }
        }
        return depth;
    }
}
